Let $f(x) = \left|\begin{array}{cccc}x & x & 1 & 2 x \\ 1 & x & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & x & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right|$.
We want to find the coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $f(x)$.
We can compute the determinant of the matrix using cofactor expansion along the first row.
$$f(x) = x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}x & 2 & -1 \\ 1 & x & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| - x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & x & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| + 1 \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & x\end{array}\right| - 2x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right|$$
We compute the $3 \times 3$ determinants:
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}x & 2 & -1 \\ 1 & x & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| &= x(x^2 - 1) - 2(x - 1) - (-1)(1 - 1) \\ &= x^3 - x - 2x + 2 + 0 \\ &= x^3 - 3x + 2\end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & x & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| &= 1(x^2 - 2) - 2(2x - 2) - (-1)(2 - 4) \\ &= x^2 - 2 - 4x + 4 + 2 \\ &= x^2 - 4x + 4\end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & x\end{array}\right| &= 1(x + 1) - x(2x - 2) - (-1)(2 - 2) \\ &= x + 1 - 2x^2 + 2x + 0 \\ &= -2x^2 + 3x + 1\end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| &= 1(1 - (-2)) - x(2 - 2) - 2(2 - 2) \\ &= 1(3) - 0 - 2(0) \\ &= 3\end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} f(x) &= x(x^3 - 3x + 2) - x(x^2 - 4x + 4) + (x^2 - 4x + 1) - 2x(3) \\ &= x^4 - 3x^2 + 2x - x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + x^2 - 4x + 1 - 6x \\ &= x^4 - x^3 + (2+4+1)x^2 + (2-4-4-6)x + 1 \\ &= x^4 - x^3 + 7x^2 - 12x + 1\end{align*}
The coefficient of $x^3$ is $-1$.

Alternatively, we can use the fact that the determinant of a matrix is the determinant of its transpose.
$$f(x)^T = \left|\begin{array}{cccc}x & 1 & 2 & 2 \\ x & x & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 & x & 1 \\ 2x & -1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right|$$
Then $f(x) = f(x)^T$.
We can also compute the determinant by cofactor expansion along the first row.
$$f(x) = x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}x & 2 & -1 \\ 1 & x & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| - x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & x & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| + 1 \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| - 2x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right|$$
We have already computed the first two determinants.
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| &= 1(1 - (-2)) - x(2 - 2) - (-1)(2 - 4) \\ &= 1(3) - 0 - (-1)(2) \\ &= 3 + 2 = 5\end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| &= 1(1 - (-2)) - x(2 - 2) - (-1)(2 - 4) \\ &= 1(3) - 0 - (-1)(2) \\ &= 3 + 2 = 5\end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} f(x) &= x(x^3 - 3x + 2) - x(x^2 - 4x + 4) + (x^2 - 4x + 1) - 2x(5) \\ &= x^4 - 3x^2 + 2x - x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + x^2 - 4x + 1 - 10x \\ &= x^4 - x^3 + (2+4+1)x^2 + (2-4-4-10)x + 1 \\ &= x^4 - x^3 + 7x^2 - 12x + 1\end{align*}
The coefficient of $x^3$ is $-1$.

