Let $f(x) = \left|\begin{array}{cccc}x & x & 1 & 2 x \\ 1 & x & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & 1 & x & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right|$.
We want to find the coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $f(x)$.
We can compute the determinant by expanding along the first row:
\begin{align*} f(x) &= x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}x & 2 & -1 \\ 1 & x & 1 \\ -1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| - x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & x & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| + 1 \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| - 2x \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| \end{align*}
We have
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}x & 2 & -1 \\ 1 & x & 1 \\ -1 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| &= x(x^2 - 1) - 2(-x - 1) - (-1)(1 - (-2)) \\ &= x^3 - x + 2x + 2 + 1 + 2 \\ &= x^3 + x + 5 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & -1 \\ 2 & x & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| &= 1(x - 2) - 2(2x - 2) - (-1)(2 - 4) \\ &= x - 2 - 4x + 4 + 2 \\ &= -3x + 4 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & -1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & x\end{array}\right| &= 1((-1) - 1) - x(2x - 2) - (-1)(2 + 2) \\ &= -2 - 2x^2 + 2x + 4 \\ &= -2x^2 + 2x + 2 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} \left|\begin{array}{ccc}1 & x & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\ 2 & -1 & 1\end{array}\right| &= 1(1 - (-2)) - x(2 - 2) - 2(2 - 2) \\ &= 3 - 0 - 0 \\ &= 3 \end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*} f(x) &= x(x^3 + x + 5) - x(-3x + 4) + 1(-2x^2 + 2x + 2) - 2x(3) \\ &= x^4 + x^2 + 5x + 3x^2 - 4x - 2x^2 + 2x + 2 - 6x \\ &= x^4 + (x^2 + 3x^2 - 2x^2) + (5x - 4x + 2x - 6x) + 2 \\ &= x^4 + 2x^2 - 3x + 2 \end{align*}
The coefficient of $x^3$ is 0.

